<GuidePage title='Applied to Tracker - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 18 · Part D: Applied</div>
  <h1>Applied to Tracker</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    Everything before this chapter was the model. This chapter is the mirror: a July 2026 audit of
    all six Next.js UIs in the Tracker monorepo, what it found, and where in this guide each fix
    lives.
  </p>

  <h2 id='why'>18.1 Why the codebase was audited</h2>
  <p>
    The audit exists to answer one question: how far the repo's actual usage of the App Router
    diverges from the model React 19 and the App Router require in order to work well? Not the model
    this guide prefers; the model the platform is built on. Deviating from it does not produce a
    different-but-valid architecture, it produces an application the framework cannot help: no
    streaming, no caching, no stability, with the costs compounding page by page (<a
      href='01-introduction.html#direction'>§1</a
    >). Before asking the team to adopt server-first patterns, the audit had to produce a specific,
    cited picture of where the codebase stands today, so that the guide's recommendations map to
    real code rather than hypotheticals, and so the fixes can be prioritized by impact instead of by
    vibes. Every finding below points at concrete files; every fix points back at the chapter that
    covers the pattern.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='voice' title="Read this in the spirit it's written">
    <p>
      This chapter is deliberately not bashful, and it is just as deliberately not a critique of
      anyone's work. Every pattern it documents was the industry-default way React was written for a
      decade, and all of it "works" today: pages render, demos pass, sprints close. The reason to
      write it down anyway is that these problems do not stay the same size. Every new page copies
      the patterns of the page before it, and every hook built around a client-side fetch becomes a
      dependency of the next feature. The gaps below will compound and get worse the longer they go
      unaddressed, and the cost of fixing them is the cheapest it will ever be right now. The focus
      is on the gaps because the gaps are the work.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='verdict'>18.2 The verdict</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The apps are SPAs wearing an App Router costume.</strong> The Next server is used almost exclusively
    to serve an empty shell and proxy API calls; nearly all data fetching, authorization, and loading-state
    management happens client-side, after hydration. In the vocabulary of <a href='02-goals.html'
      >§2</a
    >: almost nothing is treated as knowable at first render, so nearly everything a page shows is
    delivered as the unknown, with all the machinery (spinners, effects, hand-rolled loading flags)
    that
    <a href='03-mental-model.html'>§3</a> describes as the old model's tax.
  </p>

