  <h2 id='findings'>18.4 The findings</h2>
  <p>
    Eight findings, each with representative evidence and a pointer to the chapter that covers the
    correct pattern. File paths and counts are from the full audit; this is the condensed version.
  </p>

  <h3 id='finding-1'>Finding 1: All data fetching is client-side, through a self-hosted proxy</h3>
  <p>
    ojl-ui has <strong>73 route handlers</strong> under <code>src/app/api/</code>, all pass-through
    proxies that forward to the upstream API, converting the auth cookie to a Bearer header. Every
    service call in the app points at <code>/api/proxy</code>, so every read travels browser to Next
    server to upstream API: an extra network hop per request, and the server never uses any of that
    data to render. craft-profile-ui is the same pattern hand-rolled: 13 hooks, each duplicating <code
      >useState</code
    > +
    <code>useEffect</code> + manual
    <code>isLoading</code>/<code>error</code>/<code>refetch</code>, with no dedupe layer, so
    remounting any page refetches everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the fix lives:</strong> page-level reads become async server components calling the
    upstream API directly (<a href='04-ssr.html'>§4</a>), backed by a
    <code>cache()</code>-wrapped server data layer (<a href='15-dedupe-caching.html#server'>§15.2</a
    >).
  </p>

  <h3 id='finding-2'>Finding 2: "use client" poisons whole subtrees instead of leaves</h3>
  <p>
    The directive sits at page and layout level, usually just to call
    <code>useParams</code>/<code>useSearchParams</code>/<code>useRouter</code>: values a server page
    receives as props (<a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html#url-state'>§9.2</a>). The worst
    cases are <strong>client layouts</strong> on the participant and onboarding segments, which force
    everything below them client-side; one fetches
    <code>/users/me</code> after hydration just to render a first name the server already has via the
    auth cookie. Read-mostly views of 600 to 860 lines ship as client JS end to end, when only their filter
    strips and buttons need interactivity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the fix lives:</strong> pages and layouts stay server, interactivity is isolated into
    leaf client components (<a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'>§5</a>), and treating every <code
      >"use client"</code
    > as a cost to justify is the habit that keeps it that way (<a
      href='01-introduction.html#stakes'>§1</a
    >).
  </p>

  <h3 id='finding-3'>Finding 3: useEffect used for fetching, mutations, and prop-syncing</h3>
  <p>
    The audit found fetch-in-effect with hand-rolled state
    <em>next to an installed React Query</em>; a hand-written React Query (a
    <code>useState</code>+<code>useEffect</code> fetch with a module-level in-flight-promise dedup hack);
    a <strong>mutation fired from an effect</strong> (verify-email POST, with a
    <code>useRef</code> guard against StrictMode double-invoke, for a token that should be verified server-side
    and rendered as plain HTML); and prop-to-state sync effects across sibling dialogs, one carrying an
    eslint-disable to permit it. The reset-key pattern appears in exactly one file: the team knows the
    right tool and applies it inconsistently.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the fix lives:</strong> initial data moves to server components (<a
      href='04-ssr.html'>§4</a
    >), client-side needs go through one shared data layer (<a href='15-dedupe-caching.html#client'
      >§15.3</a
    >), mutations become server actions (<a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'
      >§5.7</a
    >), and React's own
    <a href='https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect' target='_blank' rel='noopener'
      >You Might Not Need an Effect</a
    >
    is the canon for everything else.
  </p>

  <h3 id='finding-4'>Finding 4: Authorization decided in the browser</h3>
  <p>
    An admin subtree is gated by a client component that fetches
    <code>/users/me/status</code> in an effect and toggles state, showing a spinner while deciding, per
    mount, with a round trip. ojl-ui has no <code>middleware.ts</code>; auth lives in the proxy
    handlers, so unauthenticated users get a full page render before any 401. Authorization is
    server-resolvable state in the purest sense: the request carries the cookie, so the server can
    decide before anything renders.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the fix lives:</strong> server layout checks with
    <code>redirect()</code>/<code>notFound()</code> plus middleware for coarse auth (<a
      href='09-server-resolvable-state.html#cookies'>§9.6</a
    >); one layout in the repo already does the cookie-presence version of this correctly.
  </p>

  <h3 id='finding-5'>Finding 5: No streaming, no route-level loading or error boundaries</h3>
  <p>
    24 of 32 ojl-ui routes, including all 15 participant and reviewer pages (the highest-traffic
    surfaces), have <strong>no loading.tsx or error.tsx</strong>. The other four apps have zero,
    total. Every loading state is a hand-rolled
    <code>isLoading</code> conditional; every error path falls through to
    <code>global-error.tsx</code> or a blank crash. No <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> streams server data
    anywhere; one page even renders <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> with no fallback.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the fix lives:</strong> containers reserve the space (<a
      href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a
    >), Suspense boundaries and route-level <code>loading</code>/<code>error</code> files own every loading
    state (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a>).
  </p>

  <h3 id='finding-6'>Finding 6: Structural waterfalls</h3>
  <p>
    Dependency chains are serialized on the client: fetch the profile, read an organization id out
    of it, then enable the dashboard query. There are
    <strong>53 <code>enabled:</code>-gated dependent queries</strong> in ojl-ui, each one a client round-trip
    that a server component would resolve in a single pass with
    <code>Promise.all</code>. A <code>consistencyToken</code> search-param hack exists to get read-your-writes
    after create: a problem <code>revalidatePath</code> plus server actions solve natively.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the fix lives:</strong> parallel fetching and minimized fetch dependencies (<a
      href='13-blocking-requests.html#parallel'>§13.2</a
    >); invalidate-don't-orchestrate for the post-mutation refresh (<a
      href='15-dedupe-caching.html#server'>§15.2</a
    >).
  </p>

  <h3 id='finding-7'>Finding 7: Caching opted out globally, then never replaced</h3>
  <p>
    Two apps set <code>export const dynamic = "force-dynamic"</code> on the
    <strong>root layout</strong> (to read boot-time env), and every fetch is
    <code>no-store</code>. That opts the apps out of static rendering, partial prerendering, and the
    data cache, while the server also fetches nothing. The only cache anywhere is React Query's
    in-memory 60-second <code>staleTime</code>, which evaporates on every full page load.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the fix lives:</strong> scope <code>force-dynamic</code> to what needs it and restore
    the layers deliberately: <code>cache()</code> per request,
    <code>"use cache"</code> across requests (<a href='15-dedupe-caching.html'>§15</a>).
  </p>

  <h3 id='finding-8'>Finding 8: App Router features missing entirely</h3>
  <p>
    No parallel routes, no intercepting routes, anywhere, despite many drawer and sheet flows that
    are pure client state and not URL-addressable (<a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html#tabs'
      >§9.3</a
    >). No server actions despite dozens of form mutations. No
    <code>not-found.tsx</code> outside the roots. These are not exotic extras; they are the primitives
    Part B and <a href='19-new-primitives.html'>§19</a> are about, and their absence is why the apps re-implement
    what the framework provides.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the fix lives:</strong> parallel routes and <code>default.tsx</code> (<a
      href='13-blocking-requests.html#parallel-routes'>§13.3</a
    >), server actions (<a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'>§5.7</a>,
    <a href='19-new-primitives.html'>§19</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id='path'>18.5 The path out</h2>
  <p>
    The fixes are ordered so that each one makes the next cheaper: server reads make leaf boundaries
    natural, leaf boundaries make streaming possible, streaming makes the hand-rolled state
    deletable.
  </p>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style='width: 4%'>#</th>
        <th>Fix</th>
        <th style='width: 26%'>Chapter</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>1</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Convert page-level reads to async server components</strong>, starting with the
          program/participant detail pages, the dashboard, and the people and organizations pages.
          Fetch upstream APIs directly with the cookie,
          <code>Promise.all</code> for parallel loads.
        </td>
        <td><a href='04-ssr.html'>§4</a>, <a href='13-blocking-requests.html'>§13</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>2</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Push "use client" to the leaves.</strong> Pages and layouts server by default; params
          and searchParams via props, not hooks. De-clientify the participant and onboarding layouts first:
          they poison everything below them.
        </td>
        <td>
          <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'>§5</a>,
          <a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>3</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Add loading.tsx and error.tsx per segment</strong> and stream with
          <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code>, then delete the hand-rolled
          <code>isLoading</code>/<code>error</code> state they replace.
        </td>
        <td>
          <a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a>,
          <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>4</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Move authorization server-side:</strong> guard components become server layout <code
            >redirect()</code
          > checks, plus middleware for coarse auth.
        </td>
        <td><a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html#cookies'>§9.6</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>5</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Kill fetch-in-useEffect.</strong> Server components for initial data; one client data
          paradigm (not three) for what genuinely loads client-side; the
          <code>key</code> prop instead of prop-sync effects; server actions plus
          <code>revalidatePath</code> instead of the <code>consistencyToken</code> hack.
        </td>
        <td>
          <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'>§5.7</a>,
          <a href='15-dedupe-caching.html#client'>§15.3</a>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>6</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Restore caching deliberately:</strong> scope <code>force-dynamic</code> to what needs
          it, <code>cache()</code> for per-request dedupe,
          <code>"use cache"</code> with tags for shared reference data.
        </td>
        <td><a href='15-dedupe-caching.html'>§15</a></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    One more time, because it is the audit's most useful fact: the repo already contains an async
    server page reading the upstream API directly with the exact same auth setup as every other app.
    Nothing on this list requires new infrastructure. It requires the mental-model shift this guide
    exists to make, applied one page at a time, starting with the pages users feel the most.
  </p>

